    "diagnosis": {
      "observation": "The village menu glyph patterns become garbled after any translated dialogue; the menu is normal before the first translated dialogue.",
      "runtime_snapshot": "Immediately after dialogue close and before opening the menu, the user observed $C4F2=0x00.",
      "code_evidence": "Dialogue cache loaders used BACKUP_ADDRESS=0xC000 for VRAM pattern backups. Existing runtime tracing records that the game uses $C000-$CFFF as a tile-font staging area and copies it to VRAM. The dialogue restore helper cleared the cache marker but did not restore the overwritten staging buffer.",
      "candidate_change": "V2.4.4 stores original dialogue glyph patterns in the expanded ROM and restores from there; those loaders no longer write backup patterns to $C000.",
      "confidence_limit": "The user confirmed the menu is still garbled in V2.4.4, so avoiding $C000 alone is insufficient; the WRAM overlap is a real conflict but not the complete regression cause.",
      "return_code_snapshots": "During the regression reproduction, $C4F0 was $BB before any Chinese dialogue and $94 after the woman dialogue; $C4F1 and $C4F2 were both $00. The shared entry return handler reads $C4F0 on a miss, making the stale return tile a likely cause."
    },
    "sameboy_observations": {
      "tested_rom_sha256": "51bbdbcd1f8729ac16c3ba7992bcff37aa66a471c80017c8f0b09f7e20cc1107",
      "menu_after_dialogue": "Still garbled after a translated dialogue."
    },
